<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Paul-Trois-Châteaux</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augusta Tricastinorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Paul-Trois-Châteaux is a small town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drome">Drôme</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>. As Augusta Tricastinorum it was the Roman capital of the Tricastini, and from late antiquity it was the seat of a bishopric, with a fine 12th-century Romanesque cathedral. The name of its district, the Tricastin, recalls the ancient people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-paul-trois-chateaux.jpg" alt="Saint-Paul-Trois-Châteaux" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Saintpaultroischateaux assumed (based on copyright claims). · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stpaul_vuegenerale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
